package com.java17.interview.prepartion;

public class HashMapConcurrentHashMap {

    /**
     * 
     * The primary difference between HashMap and ConcurrentHashMap is thread safety and locking behavior under concurrent access.While HashMap is non-synchronized and designed for optimal speed in single-threaded applications, ConcurrentHashMap is a highly scalable, thread-safe map implementation tailored for multi-threaded environments.Quick Comparison OverviewFeatureHashMapConcurrentHashMapThread SafetyNot thread-safeThread-safeLocking MechanismNo internal synchronizationFine-grained, bucket-level locking (Java 8+)Null Keys / ValuesAllows 1 null key & multiple null valuesDoes not allow any null keys or valuesIterator BehaviorFail-fast (throws exception on modification)Fail-safe / Weakly consistentPerformanceBest in single-threaded setupsBest in concurrent / multi-threaded setupsIntroduced InJDK 1.2JDK 1.5Core Differences Explained1. Thread Safety and Locking MechanismHashMap: It has no built-in synchronization. If multiple threads simultaneously modify it (e.g., executing put() or remove()), it can cause data corruption or infinite loops.ConcurrentHashMap: It achieves thread safety via a bucket-level locking system. Instead of locking the entire map object, it applies synchronization locks only to the specific array bucket being modified. Crucially, read operations (like get()) do not require locks, allowing multiple threads to read data simultaneously without blocking.2. Null Keys and Null ValuesHashMap: You can insert one null key and multiple null values into a HashMap.ConcurrentHashMap: It prohibits null keys and null values. Trying to pass a null key or value will trigger a NullPointerException. This design prevents ambiguity when checking if a key exists in concurrent environments.3. Iterator Traversal BehaviorHashMap: Its iterators are fail-fast. If any thread structurally modifies the map while another thread is iterating over it, it immediately throws a ConcurrentModificationException.ConcurrentHashMap: Its iterators are fail-safe (weakly consistent). Multiple threads can read and write to the map while an iteration is ongoing without raising any exceptions. The iterator safely processes the data state as it existed when created.4. Performance ContextHashMap: Since it has zero synchronization overhead, it provides maximum speed when used in a single thread.ConcurrentHashMap: While it carries a minimal locking overhead compared to a plain HashMap, its fine-grained architecture makes it drastically faster than a legacy Hashtable or an externally wrapped Collections.synchronizedMap() under heavy concurrent load.If you would like to look deeper into the architecture, would you like to see code examples demonstrating a multi-threaded failure in a HashMap, or explore how the internal bucketing works in Java 8+?
     */
    
}
